\title{Semiclassical Fourier Transform for Quantum Computation}

\author{Robert B.~Griffiths}
\author{Chi-Sheng Niu}
\affil{Department of Physics, Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, PA 15213, USA}
\date{arXiv:quant-ph/9511007v1, 7 Nov 1995 (PRL {\bf 76}, 3228, 1996)}

\begin{abstract}
Shor's algorithms for factorization and discrete logarithms on a quantum computer
employ Fourier transforms preceding a final measurement. It is shown that such a
Fourier transform can be carried out in a semi-classical way in which a
``classical'' (macroscopic) signal resulting from the measurement of one bit
(embodied in a two-state quantum system) is employed to determine the type of
measurement carried out on the next bit, and so forth. In this way the two-bit
gates in the Fourier transform can all be replaced by a smaller number of
one-bit gates controlled by classical signals.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Recently Shor has shown that a quantum computer would be capable of solving
certain problems much more rapidly than a conventional one. One-bit gates
are much easier to construct than two-bit gates, since the latter requires
one bit to influence another coherently without leaving a record in the
environment. In this letter we show that the QFTs which in Shor's algorithms
immediately precede a final measurement can be carried out in a
{\em semi-classical} fashion which requires no two-bit gates.

\section{Setup}

After a certain number of steps of the quantum computation the state
$|\psi\rangle$ is a coherent superposition of basis states $|a\rangle$ with
$0\le a\le q-1$, $q = 2^{s+1}$. The transform is
\begin{equation}
F|a\rangle = \frac{1}{\sqrt{q}}\sum_{c=0}^{q-1} e^{2\pi i a c/q}\,|c\rangle,
\label{eq:qft}
\end{equation}
followed by measurement of all $s+1$ bits of $c$.

Writing $a = \sum_{j=0}^{s} a_j\,2^j$ and $|a\rangle = |a_s a_{s-1}\dots a_0\rangle
= |a_s\rangle_s\otimes\dots\otimes|a_0\rangle_0$, we have
\begin{equation}
F|a\rangle = \prod_{j=0}^{s}\otimes\,|p(\phi_j)\rangle_j,
\label{eq:factored}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
|p(\phi)\rangle = \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\bigl(|0\rangle + e^{2\pi i\phi}|1\rangle\bigr),
\qquad
\phi_j = \sum_{k=0}^{s-j} a_k\,2^{\,j+k-s-1}.
\label{eq:phi}
\end{equation}

\section{Standard-QFT gates (Fig.~1)}

The top-row one-bit gates in Fig.~1 are Hadamards:
\begin{equation}
|0\rangle\mapsto\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(|0\rangle+|1\rangle)=|p(0)\rangle,\quad
|1\rangle\mapsto\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(|0\rangle-|1\rangle)=|p(1/2)\rangle.
\label{eq:H}
\end{equation}
A two-bit gate labelled with integer $m$ acts as
\begin{equation}
|00\rangle\mapsto|00\rangle,\ |01\rangle\mapsto|01\rangle,\ |10\rangle\mapsto|10\rangle,\
|11\rangle\mapsto e^{2\pi i/2^m}|11\rangle.
\label{eq:CP}
\end{equation}

\section{Semiclassical QFT (Fig.~2)}

Since a control bit passes through the two-bit gate unchanged, if the final
measurement of $c_0$ yields $1$ we can equally say the bit was $|1\rangle$
already at point $B$ (just after its Hadamard). Hence $c_0$ can be measured
at $B$, provided the classical result is used to determine the action of a
set of one-bit gates on the remaining bits. Applying this reasoning
throughout Fig.~1 yields Fig.~2: all two-bit gates are eliminated, and
each ``box'' does the following.

\begin{enumerate}
\item Apply
\begin{equation}
|0\rangle\mapsto\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(|0\rangle+|1\rangle),\qquad
|1\rangle\mapsto e^{2\pi i\phi}\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}(|0\rangle-|1\rangle),
\label{eq:box-unitary}
\end{equation}
where $\phi$ is the classical phase transmitted from the previous box.
\item Measure the bit to get $c\in\{0,1\}$.
\item Emit two classical signals: $c$ and the next-phase
\begin{equation}
\phi' = \phi/2 + c/4.
\label{eq:phi-update}
\end{equation}
The very first box uses $\phi=0$.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Statistical equivalence.} The paper argues (via consistent
histories) that the measurement distribution over the $s+1$ classical
outcome bits is {\em identical} for the standard circuit (Fig.~1
followed by measurement) and the semiclassical circuit (Fig.~2). This
is the central quantitative claim, which the present replication tests.

\section{Gate count}

Standard-QFT circuit (Fig.~1) on $n=s+1$ qubits: $n$ Hadamards, $n(n-1)/2$
two-bit controlled-phase gates. Semiclassical circuit (Fig.~2): $n$
Hadamards, $n$ single-bit measurements, and $n(n-1)/2$ single-bit
phase gates each classically conditioned on an earlier measurement
outcome. Zero coherent two-bit gates.

\section{Conclusions}

(i) A new class of quantum-computation technique in which classical
measurement outcomes are fed forward to control later quantum gates.
(ii) A large experimental simplification of Shor's algorithm.
(iii) A clean interpretation in the consistent-histories formalism.

